The table of named groups (the name table of the compiled pattern) was not
initialised after its allocation: add_name() compares the names of the
entries past their ends (and an entry can grow later), so a pattern with
named groups whose names begin alike, (?<f>)(?<fir>), read uninitialised
memory while compiling (found with MemorySanitizer; Exim bug 2661).
Initialised just after the allocation now.

Petr Pisar's fix as Fedora carries it on 8.45 (pcre 8.45,
pcre-8.44-Inicialize-name-table-memory-region.patch, f0bb9e8b of 19.10.2020);
the release of PCRE1 after 8.45 never came.

--- pcre_compile.c.orig
+++ pcre_compile.c
@@ -9423,6 +9423,11 @@
   goto PCRE_EARLY_ERROR_RETURN;
   }
 
+/* Initialize the memory. Name table entry values are accessed past their ends
+ * (e.g. in add_name()) when comparing the values. Also a size of the entry can
+ * grow later. It's safer to initialize here than to hunt the gaps later. */
+memset(re, 0, size);
+
